  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe heat wave will continue with our region remaining under the \ninfluence of an upper level ridge.  A Heat Advisory remains in \neffect through Sunday as high temperatures continue to reach \nnear record to record readings in the middle 90s to near 100 \ndegrees. \n\nA weak boundary to our north is expected to sag far enough \nsouthwest late today and trigger isolated showers and \nthunderstorms across northwest and eastern portions of the PAH \nforecast area (fa). The boundary will retreat back northeast \nlate tonight, but a stray shower of storms is not out of the \nquestion Saturday afternoon in portions of southwest Indiana.\n\nThe upper ridge will gradually break down over the weekend, and \nmodels bring a cold front slowly through the middle Mississippi\nand lower Ohio valley late Sunday into Monday. Winds will shift\nto the west then north Sunday afternoon into Sunday night. \nChances of showers and storms will slowly spread into northern \nand western portions of the PAH fa late Sunday night into Monday\nmorning, with chances increasing and spreading southeast by \nMonday afternoon. Monday afternoon chances will range from \n20-40% across west Kentucky, to 40-60% chances across the rest \nof the area, then 30-50% chances will continue into Monday \nevening. 20-40% chances will taper off from northwest to \nsoutheast late Monday night through Tuesday. \n\nThe passage of the cold front will finally bring welcome relief\nfrom the heat, with highs Monday ranging from the upper 80s \nnorthwest to middle 90s southeast, then Tuesday from the middle\n70s northwest to middle 80s southeast. Dry conditions and near \nto even a little below normal temperatures will then persist \nWednesday into Thursday with highs in the middle 70s to around \n80 degrees and very low humidity.",
